            <ArticleTitle>Do stress echocardiography and myocardial perfusion imaging have the same ability to identify the low-risk patient with known or suspected coronary artery disease?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1050-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although both stress myocardial perfusion imaging and echocardiography may have significant prognostic value, the annual death/myocardial infarction rate appears to be much higher in patients with a negative stress echocardiogram. This may greatly limit its ability to act as a &quot;gatekeeper&quot; for additional invasive and interventional procedures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
